---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MatrixCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc2/MatrixCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="matrix-calculator">
  <MatrixCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the matrix calculator</h2>
    <p>
      Pick an operation, set the size of each matrix (up to 4 × 4) and type the numbers into the cells. The
      matrix calculator works out the answer as you type and writes out every step. For a 2 × 2 matrix it
      also animates what the matrix does to the plane; for the other operations it marks the cells that make
      each cell of the result.
    </p>

    <h2>The formulas</h2>
    <pre><code>A + B:   cᵢⱼ = aᵢⱼ + bᵢⱼ            (same size)
A × B:   cᵢⱼ = Σₖ aᵢₖ bₖⱼ           (columns of A = rows of B)
kA:      cᵢⱼ = k aᵢⱼ
Aᵀ:      cᵢⱼ = aⱼᵢ
det, 2×2:  ad − bc
inverse, 2×2:  (1 ÷ (ad − bc)) × [d −b; −c a]</code></pre>
    <p>
      For 3 × 3 the matrix calculator expands the determinant along the first row. For 4 × 4 it reduces the
      matrix to upper triangular form and multiplies the diagonal. Inverses of 3 × 3 and 4 × 4 matrices come
      from Gauss-Jordan elimination on [A | I]. The rank is the number of pivots in the reduced row echelon
      form.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example: a 2 × 2 determinant and inverse</h2>
    <p>
      For A = [3 1; 1 2], det A = 3 × 2 − 1 × 1 = 5. The inverse is (1 ÷ 5) × [2 −1; −1 3] = [0.4 −0.2;
      −0.2 0.6]. Check: A × A⁻¹ = [3 × 0.4 + 1 × (−0.2), …] = [1 0; 0 1].
    </p>
    <p>
      The animation shows the same number as an area. A sends the point (1, 0) to (3, 1) and (0, 1) to
      (1, 2). The unit square becomes the parallelogram with those two sides, and its area is 5.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example: a product</h2>
    <p>
      [1 2; 3 4] × [5 6; 7 8]: the top left cell is row 1 times column 1, 1 × 5 + 2 × 7 = 19. The others are
      22, 43 and 50. Swap the matrices and the result is [23 34; 31 46], which shows that A × B and B × A
      differ.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Matrices up to 4 × 4, with real numbers. Fractions are entered as decimals.</li>
      <li>Results are floating point. Values within 10⁻¹² of a whole number are shown as that whole number.</li>
      <li>A matrix that is nearly singular can give an inverse with very large values that are not reliable.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
